course: dictionary
lesson: 2743
title: Disadvantage: when the odds are against you
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A disadvantage is something that makes things harder or less good. When you look for clues, try the opposite word, advantage. If something is an advantage, it helps you. If it is a disadvantage, it gets in your way. «Having no garden is a disadvantage when you want a pet rabbit.» The sentence tells you that not having a garden makes the rabbit harder to keep.
explain: To be at a disadvantage means to have less chance than others. You do not need to be unlucky to be at a disadvantage. «Playing with a sprained ankle put the team at a disadvantage.» The team had fewer chances to win, even though they were still trying their best.
explain: Disadvantage can also be a verb. To disadvantage someone is to put them in a worse position. Look at the ending too: «disadvantaged» is the form you see when someone already has the disadvantage. «The unfair rules disadvantaged the younger children.» The rules made it harder for them, so they were in a worse position.
explain: Writers use this word when they compare things, for example the advantages and disadvantages of a plan. Good writers look at both sides before they decide. When you meet this word, ask yourself: what is making this harder, and who is in the worse position?
fact: A disadvantage is something that makes things harder or less good.
fact: The opposite of disadvantage is advantage.
fact: To be at a disadvantage means to have less chance than other people.
fact: «Disadvantaged background» means someone grew up poor.
fact: To disadvantage someone means to put them in a worse position.
exercise: «Having no garden is a disadvantage when you want a pet rabbit.» What is a disadvantage? | *Something that makes things harder | A type of fruit | A kind of game | Something that makes things easier and better
exercise: «Playing with a sprained ankle put the team at a disadvantage.» What does at a disadvantage mean? | *Having less chance than others | Having more chance than others | Winning the match easily | Having a lot of fun
exercise: Which word is the opposite of disadvantage? | Problem | Trouble | *Advantage | Danger
test: «The unfair rules disadvantaged the younger children.» What does disadvantaged mean here? | Moved to a new school | Made to laugh | Given extra prizes | *Put in a worse position
test: When you write about a plan, what should you do? | Write only the good points | Write nothing at all | *Write one advantage and one disadvantage | Write only the bad points
exam: «She grew up in a disadvantaged background, with very little money.» What does this sentence tell us? | She grew up on a farm | *She had less money and fewer chances than many others | She grew up without any friends | She grew up very rich
